g.\n\nI've counted cards. There are four unknown 2s and Aces, which is dangerous, so I want to make sure I don't burn all my big cards. My teammate has 17 cards, and there's a good chance they have something. I can advance the game.\n\nI also can't split the 888, which means I can still build my hand. I'm not going to play QQ on a 33. I should clear my hand as best as possible. The goal is to get rid of cards, and 44 will do the trick. The best thing to do is to play ♠4 ♣4.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord has opened with the lowest possible pair (33). As the first Peasant to act, my primary goal is usually to either block the Landlord with a high card or efficiently clear my own weak cards. My hand contains a mix of singles, pairs, and a triplet, but lacks the 2s and Aces needed for sustained control (though I have the Red Joker). Playing my high pair (QQ) now would be too aggressive given the weakness of the rest of my hand. Playing my lowest pair (44) is the most efficient move: it beats the Landlord\'s play, clears my smallest trash cards, and preserves my hand structure (keeping the option for a straight or triplet of 8s). It also allows the game to continue at a level where my teammate might be able to contribute without me wasting high-value cards prematurely.\n\nReason: I possess a pair of 4s (♠4 ♣4), which is the most economical way to beat the Landlord\'s pair of 3s. This move clears my lowest cards while maintaining my hand\'s structure (saving the QQ for defense and keeping the 888/straight possibilities intact).\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠4", "♣4"]}'}
